t":false,"url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Corporate, Performance and Budget Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Property assets showing \u00a3430k budget pressure from reduced accommodation income and increased facilities costs","lead":"Deteriorating property asset income signals potential issues with lettings, occupancy, or asset management\u2014all key indicators of financial resilience relevant to lenders. Rising facilities costs suggest deferred maintenance or changing property utilisation.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58742/58742.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"The Delivery of the Transformation and Savings Programme Reserve is being drawn down by \u00a3640k, suggesting planned transformation savings may not be delivering as forecast.","lead":"If a council's transformation programme is not delivering planned savings, the council faces an underlying structural budget gap that reserves alone cannot address sustainably.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 3","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58744/58744.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Business Rates Equalisation Reserve is being drawn down by \u00a33.264m (97% of the opening balance), indicating business rates income has fallen short of forecast.","lead":"Business rates income volatility is a material risk for local authorities. If forecast income is not materializing, it suggests either the forecast was optimistic or the local economy is weaker than expected, both of which affect affordability.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 3","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58744/58744.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58742/58742.pdf","headline":"Property assets showing \u00a3430k budget pressure from reduced accommodation income and increased facilities costs","detail":"The Property directorate forecast is \u00a3430k over budget (\u00a3652k outturn against \u00a3222k budget). Accommodation income is expected to be \u00a3150k lower than budgeted (\u00a3243k deficit against \u00a3393k budgeted deficit). Facilities costs are forecast \u00a3134k above budget at \u00a3665k. Town Centre spending is also \u00a368k over budget at \u00a3162k. This is the council's single largest departmental overspend despite overall council position forecasting underspend.","category":"income","matters":"Deteriorating property asset income signals potential issues with lettings, occupancy, or asset management\u2014all key indicators of financial resilience relevant to lenders. Rising facilities costs suggest deferred maintenance or changing property utilisation.","figures":"Property overspend \u00a3430k (budget \u00a3222k, forecast outturn \u00a3652k); Accommodation income variance \u00a3150k; Facilities overspend \u00a3134k; Town Centre overspend \u00a368k","quote":"Property (Part of Planning and Property) Total 222 222 652 430","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 3","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58744/58744.pdf","headline":"The Delivery of the Transformation and Savings Programme Reserve is being drawn down by \u00a3640k, suggesting planned transformation savings may not be delivering as forecast.","detail":"A \u00a3697k reserve specifically set aside for transformation and savings programme delivery is being reduced to zero uncommitted balance through transfers. This suggests the council's transformation programme, which was expected to generate savings, is either delayed or not generating the anticipated benefits.","category":"savings","matters":"If a council's transformation programme is not delivering planned savings, the council faces an underlying structural budget gap that reserves alone cannot address sustainably.","figures":"Delivery of the Transformation and Savings Programme Reserve: \u00a3697k opening; \u00a3640k transfers from; \u00a30 uncommitted","quote":"Delivery of the Transformation and Savings Programme Reserve (697) 57 (640) (640)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 3","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58744/58744.pdf","headline":"Business Rates Equalisation Reserve is being drawn down by \u00a33.264m (97% of the opening balance), indicating business rates income has fallen short of forecast.","detail":"The Business Rates Equalisation Reserve opens at \u00a33,279k and is forecast to be reduced to zero uncommitted balance, with \u00a33,264k transferred from reserves. This is the largest single reserve deployment and suggests the council's business rates income assumptions have not held.","category":"income","matters":"Business rates income volatility is a material risk for local authorities. If forecast income is not materializing, it suggests either the forecast was optimistic or the local economy is weaker than expected, both of which affect affordability.","figures":"Business Rates Equalisation Reserve: \u00a33,279k opening; \u00a33,264k transfers from reserves; \u00a30 uncommitted at year end","quote":"Business Rates Equalisation Reserve (3,279) 15 (3,264) (3,264)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 3","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58744/58744.pdf","headline":"Reserves are being significantly depleted to fund the 2026/27 budget, with total reserves falling from \u00a321.2m to \u00a319.7m.","detail":"Total reserves are forecast to decline by \u00a31.6m across the year. Earmarked reserves fall from \u00a316.9m to \u00a314.8m, with uncommitted reserves at 31 March 2027 standing at \u00a312.2m. Multiple specific reserves show full or near-full drawdown to fund operations, rather than being retained as contingency.","category":"reserves","matters":"Lenders care whether reserves are stabilizing or being consumed; sustained reserve depletion suggests the council cannot balance its budget from recurring income and is drawing down a finite buffer.","figures":"Total Reserves: \u00a321,223k (opening) to \u00a319,667k (forecast close); Total Earmarked Reserves: \u00a316,890k to \u00a314,803k; Uncommitted Reserves at 31 March 2027: \u00a312,196k","quote":"Balance at 01 April 2026 (4,333) to Balance at 31 March 2027 (4,864) for General Fund; Total Reserves - General and Earmarked (21,223) to (19,667)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Report Q1","paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58741/58741.pdf","headline":"Earmarked reserves declining from \u00a316.890m to \u00a314.803m, with \u00a30.681m used to finance capital programme","detail":"The council is using reserves to fund capital spend (\u00a30.681m) and drawing down \u00a31.475m of specific earmarked reserves for various programmes. This is a \u00a32.087m reduction in discretionary reserve balances in a single year. Combined with the unresolved 2027/28 budget gap, this trajectory indicates reserves may be used to bridge future funding shortfalls.","category":"reserves","matters":"Reserve depletion to fund capital and current spending, combined with an unresolved budget gap ahead, suggests the council may not have headroom to absorb future shocks without either deeper savings or borrowing","figures":"Earmarked reserves 1 April 2026: \u00a316.890m; closing 31 March 2027: \u00a314.803m; use for capital financing: \u00a30.681m; use of other specific earmarked reserves: \u00a31.475m","quote":"Taking account of the adjustments highlighted in Appendix 3, the level of Earmarked reserves held for discretionary use by the Council at 31 March 2027 is (\u00a314.803m) compared to a balance of (\u00a316.890m) at 31 March 2026.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"South Ribble","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Property assets showing \u00a3430k budget pressure from reduced accommodation income and increased facilities costs","lead":"Deteriorating property asset income signals potential issues with lettings, occupancy, or asset management\u2014all key indicators of financial resilience relevant to lenders. Rising facilities costs suggest deferred maintenance or changing property utilisation.","also":["8 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Governance Committee on 2026-09-22","n_findings":9,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix 1","top_paper_url":"https://southribble.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s58742/58742.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":50,"news_drafts_total":3}}